### What exactly happens when a consumer crashes?

With at-least-once delivery and manual acknowledgment, the consumer receives a message, processes it, and only then acknowledges. If it crashes before acknowledging, the broker redelivers the message.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.Acknowledgment;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransferEventConsumer {

    private final TransferProcessor processor;

    public TransferEventConsumer(TransferProcessor processor) {
        this.processor = processor;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-events", groupId = "credit-group")
    public void onMessage(String payload, Acknowledgment ack) {
        // 1. Process the business logic
        processor.creditRecipient(payload);

        // 2. Acknowledge ONLY after commit
        ack.acknowledge();
        System.out.println("Message acknowledged after successful processing");

        // If crash happens between 1 and 2:
        //   -> Kafka sees no ack -> redelivers the message
        //   -> Message is processed AGAIN (must be idempotent!)
    }
}
```

```text
Crash scenarios:
  Crash BEFORE processing:  message redelivered, no harm
  Crash DURING processing:  partial work + redelivery = DUPLICATE risk
  Crash AFTER processing, BEFORE ack: work done + redelivery = DUPLICATE
  -> Idempotency handles both duplicate scenarios
```

### How does idempotency make redelivery safe?

Before processing, check whether this message ID has already been handled. If yes, skip and acknowledge.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class IdempotentCreditService {

    private final ProcessedMessageRepository processedRepo;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public IdempotentCreditService(ProcessedMessageRepository processedRepo,
                                    AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.processedRepo = processedRepo;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void creditRecipient(String messageId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Idempotency check: has this message been processed?
        if (processedRepo.existsById(messageId)) {
            System.out.println("Message " + messageId + " already processed, skipping");
            return;
        }

        // Process the credit
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(account);

        // Record that this message was processed (in same transaction)
        processedRepo.save(new ProcessedMessage(messageId));
        System.out.println("Credited " + amount + " to " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (first delivery):
Credited 50000.00 to SAV2002

Expected output (redelivery after crash):
Message MSG-001 already processed, skipping
```

### How does the dead-letter queue handle poison messages?

Some messages will never succeed. After bounded retries, route them to a DLQ for inspection.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DefaultErrorHandler;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.util.backoff.FixedBackOff;

@Configuration
public class KafkaErrorConfig {

    @Bean
    public DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(
            KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        // After 3 retries with 1-second backoff, send to DLQ
        DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer =
                new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(kafkaTemplate);
        return new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3));
    }
}
```

```text
Poison message handling:
  Attempt 1: NullPointerException (account_id is null)
  Attempt 2: NullPointerException
  Attempt 3: NullPointerException
  -> Sent to transfer-events.DLT (dead letter topic)
  -> Normal messages continue processing
  -> Ops team inspects DLT, fixes data, replays if needed
```

### How does the transactional outbox protect the producer?

The dual-write problem: update DB then publish event. If the process crashes between them, the event is lost. The outbox pattern writes both in one transaction.

```java
package com.bank.outbox;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class OutboxTransferService {

    private final TransferRepository transferRepo;
    private final OutboxRepository outboxRepo;

    public OutboxTransferService(TransferRepository transferRepo,
                                  OutboxRepository outboxRepo) {
        this.transferRepo = transferRepo;
        this.outboxRepo = outboxRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void executeTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Both in ONE transaction: crash between them is impossible
        transferRepo.save(new Transfer(from, to, amount));
        outboxRepo.save(new OutboxEvent("TRANSFER_COMPLETED",
                "{\"from\":\"" + from + "\",\"to\":\"" + to + "\"}"));
        System.out.println("Transfer + outbox event committed atomically");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transfer + outbox event committed atomically

Outbox relay publishes the event to Kafka separately.
Crash between DB write and Kafka publish is IMPOSSIBLE.
```

### How does a circuit breaker protect the consumer?

If the consumer calls a downstream that is down, retrying every message wastes resources. A circuit breaker fails fast during a cooldown period.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class FraudCheckClient {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "fraudCheck", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public String checkFraud(String accountId) {
        return externalFraudService.check(accountId);
    }

    public String fallback(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        System.out.println("Fraud service down, marking for manual review");
        return "MANUAL_REVIEW";
    }
}
```

```text
Without circuit breaker:
  Fraud service down -> every message retries fraud call 3x
  -> 10,000 messages * 3 retries = 30,000 wasted calls

With circuit breaker:
  Fraud service down -> circuit opens after 10 failures
  -> Remaining messages get fallback instantly
  -> Fraud service recovers, circuit half-opens, tests, closes
```

### How does a Saga handle multi-step failures?

When a crash happens partway through a sequence of local transactions across services, a Saga runs compensating transactions to undo the committed steps.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

public class SagaCompensation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Multi-step transfer Saga:");
        System.out.println("  Step 1: Debit from account (committed)");
        System.out.println("  Step 2: Credit to account (committed)");
        System.out.println("  Step 3: Notify recipient (FAILED - crash)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Compensation:");
        System.out.println("  C2: Reverse credit to account");
        System.out.println("  C1: Reverse debit from account");
        System.out.println("  Result: money back to original state");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Multi-step transfer Saga:
  Step 1: Debit from account (committed)
  Step 2: Credit to account (committed)
  Step 3: Notify recipient (FAILED - crash)

Compensation:
  C2: Reverse credit to account
  C1: Reverse debit from account
  Result: money back to original state
```

### How do these mechanisms compose?

Each mechanism covers a different failure mode. Together they provide end-to-end reliability.

| Mechanism | What it guarantees |
|---|---|
| Transactional outbox | Event is never lost (producer side) |
| Manual ack + retry | Message is delivered and retried (broker side) |
| Idempotency | Redelivery is safe (consumer side) |
| Dead-letter queue | Poison messages do not block good ones |
| Circuit breaker | Failing dependency does not drain threads |
| Saga | Multi-step workflow stays consistent |

```text
Composition:
  Outbox -> guarantees event published
  Kafka at-least-once -> guarantees delivery
  Idempotency -> makes redelivery safe
  DLQ -> contains poison
  Circuit breaker -> protects dependencies
  Saga -> keeps multi-step workflow consistent
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The processed-message table must be cleaned up periodically with a TTL. The DLQ is not a trash can but an inspection queue. The ack-mode in Spring Kafka is configured as MANUAL for at-least-once guarantees.

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    consumer:
      enable-auto-commit: false
    listener:
      ack-mode: MANUAL
```

```text
Senior details:
  - enable-auto-commit: false + ack-mode: MANUAL = at-least-once
  - Processed message table: TTL 7 days, indexed by message_id
  - DLQ: alert on volume, inspect daily, replay after fix
  - Outbox: poll-based for simplicity, Debezium CDC for low latency
```
